{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LQin1wTG6oqR8v2BMtS2kP","type":"tool_result","content":"280\tpub const QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN: usize = 16;\n281\t\n282\t// The AEAD wrappers (`aead_encrypt_generic`, `aead_decrypt_generic`,\n283\t// `aead_encrypt_mp` / `aead_decrypt_mp`, `aead_integrity_limit`,\n284\t// `aead_confidentiality_limit`) are gone -- their behaviour is the\n285\t// `crate::tls::PacketKey` trait.  Backends supply concrete\n286\t// implementations.  Multipath variants fold into the same trait by\n287\t// XORing the path id into the nonce inside the implementor.\n288\t//\n289\t// `aead_free` and `cipher_free` are gone -- `Drop` on the boxed\n290\t// trait object replaces them.\n291\t//\n292\t// `pn_iv_size` and `pn_encrypt` are gone -- header protection\n293\t// (the only consumer) is implemented in `crate::header_protection`\n294\t// and exposed through `crate::tls::HeaderKey`.\n295\t\n296\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n297\t// Initial-secret derivation.\n298\t//\n299\t// RFC 9001 fixes the initial-secret HKDF to SHA-256 (the salt is the\n300\t// version-specific 20-byte constant).  Both helpers therefore commit\n301\t// to SHA-256 internally — no cipher-suite parameter needed (the C\n302\t// API took `&PtlsCipherSuite` purely for parity with later epoch\n303\t// derivations).  `master_secret` / `client_secret` / `server_secret`\n304\t// must be 32 bytes (`Sha256::output_size()`); a wider buffer is\n305\t// fine (caller-provided slots stay flexible).\n306\t\n307\t/// Derive the per-connection-ID initial master secret.  C:\n308\t/// `setup_initial_master_secret`.\n309\tpub fn setup_initial_master_secret(\n310\t    _salt: &[u8],\n311\t    _initial_connection_id: ConnectionId,\n312\t    _master_secret: &mut [u8],\n313\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n314\t    todo!()\n315\t}\n316\t\n317\t/// Derive client/server initial secrets from the master secret.\n318\t/// `client_secret` and `server_secret` are filled in place.  C:\n319\t/// `setup_initial_secrets`.\n320\tpub fn setup_initial_secrets(\n321\t    _master_secret: &[u8],\n322\t    _client_secret: &mut [u8],\n323\t    _server_secret: &mut [u8],\n324\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n325\t    todo!()\n326\t}\n327\t\n328\timpl Connection {\n329\t    /// Set up this connection's per-epoch initial AEAD / PN\n330\t    /// encryption contexts from the connection's initial CID.  C:\n331\t    /// `setup_initial_traffic_keys`.\n332\t    pub fn setup_initial_traffic_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n333\t        todo!()\n334\t    }\n335\t}\n336\t\n337\t/// Output bundle from [`Quic::initial_aead_context`].  C returned\n338\t/// the AEAD and PN contexts through `void**` out-pointers; the Rust\n339\t/// shape collapses both into a struct so the function signature is\n340\t/// one-out, one-return.\n341\tpub struct InitialAeadContext {\n342\t    /// Owned AEAD context for packet protection.\n343\t    pub aead_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>,\n344\t    /// Owned header-protection context.\n345\t    pub pn_enc_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>,\n346\t}\n347\t\n348\timpl Quic {\n349\t    /// Derive an AEAD + PN context for the initial encryption\n350\t    /// level.  `version_index` selects the QUIC version's salt and\n351\t    /// prefix label; `is_client` and `is_enc` pick the\n352\t    /// client/server and encrypt/decrypt directions.  C:\n353\t    /// `get_initial_aead_context`.\n354\t    pub fn initial_aead_context(\n355\t        &mut self,\n356\t        _version_index: i32,\n357\t        _initial_connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n358\t        _is_client: bool,\n359\t        _is_enc: bool,\n360\t    ) -> Result<InitialAeadContext, Error> {\n361\t        todo!()\n362\t    }\n363\t}\n364\t\n365\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n366\t// Application-secret access and rotation.\n367\t//\n368\t// `get_app_secret` returns one of the two\n369\t// `app_secret_*` buffers stashed inside `tls_ctx_t`.  The\n370\t// C signature returned a `uint8_t*` into that buffer; in Rust the\n371\t// natural shape is a borrowed slice carrying the digest length\n372\t// (callers consult `get_app_secret_size` for the length\n373\t// today, which is redundant once the borrow encodes it).  Phase 3\n374\t// may collapse the size accessor.\n375\t\n376\timpl Connection {\n377\t    /// Return a borrow of the app-data traffic secret stored in\n378\t    /// this connection's TLS context for the chosen direction.  C:\n379\t    /// `get_app_secret`.\n380\t    pub fn app_secret(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) -> &mut [u8] {\n381\t        todo!()\n382\t    }\n383\t\n384\t    /// Length (bytes) of the app-data traffic secret — the digest\n385\t    /// size of the negotiated cipher's hash.  C:\n386\t    /// `get_app_secret_size`.  Phase 3 may collapse this accessor\n387\t    /// since [`Connection::app_secret`] already returns a sized slice.\n388\t    pub fn app_secret_size(&self) -> usize {\n389\t        todo!()\n390\t    }\n391\t\n392\t    /// Compute the post-rotation AEAD + PN contexts and stash them\n393\t    /// in `crypto_context_new`.  C: `compute_new_rotated_keys`.\n394\t    pub fn compute_new_rotated_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n395\t        todo!()\n396\t    }\n397\t\n398\t    /// Promote `crypto_context_new` to the active\n399\t    /// `crypto_context[3]` slot, demoting the previous keys.  C:\n400\t    /// `apply_rotated_keys`.\n401\t    pub fn apply_rotated_keys(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) {\n402\t        todo!()\n403\t    }\n404\t}\n405\t\n406\t/// Rotate the application traffic secret in place using the\n407\t/// version-specific traffic-update label.  The active hash\n408\t/// algorithm is supplied through a fresh [`digest::DynDigest`]\n409\t/// instance (callers obtain one from\n410\t/// [`crate::tls::Session`]).  C: `rotate_app_secret`.\n411\tpub fn rotate_app_secret(\n412\t    _hash: &mut dyn digest::DynDigest,\n413\t    _secret: &mut [u8],\n414\t    _traffic_update_label: &str,\n415\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n416\t    todo!()\n417\t}\n418\t\n419\timpl CryptoContext {\n420\t    /// Free every AEAD / PN-encryption slot held by this crypto\n421\t    /// context.  Called from key-rotation paths to recycle the\n422\t    /// underlying tls handles without dropping the parent\n423\t    /// [`Connection`], so this is *not* an `impl Drop` — Phase 3 may\n424\t    /// still install one for the parent-drop path.  C:\n425\t    /// `crypto_context_free`.\n426\t    pub fn free_handles(&mut self) {\n427\t        todo!()\n428\t    }\n429\t}\n430\t\n431\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n432\t// Test helpers (still part of the public API surface).\n433\t//\n434\t// These two helpers build standalone AEAD / PN-encryption contexts\n435\t// from a raw secret.  They're used by the test suite to mock\n436\t// crypto state.  The Phase 2 trait family ([`crate::tls`]) is the\n437\t// return shape.\n438\t\n439\t/// Construct an AEAD context backed by AES128-GCM-SHA256 from a\n440\t/// raw secret + prefix label.  C: `setup_test_aead_context`.\n441\tpub fn setup_test_aead_context(\n442\t    _is_encrypt: bool,\n443\t    _secret: &[u8],\n444\t    _prefix_label: &str,\n445\t) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n446\t    todo!()\n447\t}\n448\t\n449\t/// Construct a PN-encryption context for tests.  C:\n450\t/// `pn_enc_create_for_test`.\n451\tpub fn pn_enc_create_for_test(\n452\t    _secret: &[u8],\n453\t    _prefix_label: &str,\n454\t) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>> {\n455\t    todo!()\n456\t}\n457\t\n458\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n459\t// Reset secret and verify-certificate management.\n460\t\n461\timpl Quic {\n462\t    /// Compute the 16-byte reset secret tied to `connection_id` using this\n463\t    /// context's reset seed.  C: `create_connection_id_reset_secret`.\n464\t    pub fn create_connection_id_reset_secret(\n465\t        &mut self,\n466\t        _cnx_id: &ConnectionId,\n467\t        _reset_secret: &mut [u8; RESET_SECRET_SIZE],\n468\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n469\t        todo!()\n470\t    }\n471\t\n472\t    // The C `tls_set_verify_certificate_callback` /\n473\t    // `dispose_verify_certificate_callback` pair is folded into\n474\t    // [`crate::tls::TlsCallbacks`] — applications install a single\n475\t    // callbacks bundle on the QUIC context and override\n476\t    // `verify_certificate` to plug in their own logic.\n477\t\n478\t    /// Toggle whether the server requires client certificates.  C\n479\t    /// took an `int`; promoted to `bool`.  C:\n480\t    /// `tls_set_client_authentication`.\n481\t    pub fn tls_set_client_authentication(&mut self, _client_authentication: bool) {\n482\t        todo!()\n483\t    }\n484\t\n485\t    /// Report whether client authentication is currently required.\n486\t    /// C: `tls_client_authentication_activated`.\n487\t    pub fn tls_client_authentication_activated(&self) -> bool {\n488\t        todo!()\n489\t    }\n490\t\n491\t    /// Toggle whether tls exposes its exporter API on this master\n492\t    /// context.  C: `tls_set_use_exporter`.\n493\t    pub fn tls_set_use_exporter(&mut self, _use_exporter: bool) {\n494\t        todo!()\n495\t    }\n496\t}\n497\t\n498\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n499\t// Retry tokens.\n500\t//\n501\t// `server_decrypt_retry_token`,\n502\t// `prepare_retry_token`, and\n503\t// `verify_retry_token` were the three token entry points\n504\t// declared in this header.  All three take a `struct sockaddr*`\n505\t// peer address (mapped to [`SocketAddr`]) and produce / consume a\n506\t// caller-allocated `token` buffer.\n507\t\n508\t/// Output bundle from [`server_decrypt_retry_token`].  C\n509\t/// returned the new-token flag through `int*` and the plaintext\n510\t/// length through `size_t*`; collapsing them into a struct keeps\n511\t/// the result one piece.  The plaintext is written into the\n512\t/// caller's `text` slice; `text_length` is how many bytes were\n513\t/// actually written.\n514\tpub struct DecryptedRetryToken {\n515\t    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token` out-parameter; promoted\n516\t    /// to `bool`.\n517\t    pub is_new_token: bool,\n518\t    /// Mirrors the C `size_t* text_length` out-parameter.\n519\t    pub text_length: usize,\n520\t}\n521\t\n522\timpl Quic {\n523\t    /// Decrypt a retry token and verify its peer-address binding.\n524\t    /// The plaintext is written into `text`; the result describes\n525\t    /// how many bytes were written and whether the token was a \"new\n526\t    /// token\" or a classic retry token.  C:\n527\t    /// `server_decrypt_retry_token`.\n528\t    pub fn server_decrypt_retry_token(\n529\t        &mut self,\n530\t        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n531\t        _token: &[u8],\n532\t        _text: &mut [u8],\n533\t    ) -> Result<DecryptedRetryToken, Error> {\n534\t        todo!()\n535\t    }\n536\t\n537\t    /// Construct a retry / new token signed for `addr_peer`.\n538\t    /// Returns the number of bytes written into `token`; `token_max`\n539\t    /// is `token.len()`.  C: `prepare_retry_token`.\n540\t    pub fn prepare_retry_token(\n541\t        &mut self,\n542\t        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n543\t        _current_time: Instant,\n544\t        _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n545\t        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n546\t        _initial_pn: u32,\n547\t        _token: &mut [u8],\n548\t    ) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n549\t        todo!()\n550\t    }\n551\t}\n552\t\n553\t/// Output bundle from [`verify_retry_token`].  Mirrors the\n554\t/// `int* is_new_token` and `ConnectionId* odcid`\n555\t/// out-parameters of the C signature.\n556\tpub struct VerifiedRetryToken {\n557\t    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token`; promoted to `bool`.\n558\t    pub is_new_token: bool,\n559\t    /// Original Destination Connection ID extracted from the\n560\t    /// token.  Empty (`id_len == 0`) for \"new tokens\".\n561\t    pub odcid: ConnectionId,\n562\t}\n563\t\n564\timpl Quic {\n565\t    /// Verify a retry / new token presented by the peer.  Returns\n566\t    /// the extracted bundle when the token is fresh, address-bound,\n567\t    /// and (for retry tokens) RCID-matched.  C:\n568\t    /// `verify_retry_token`.\n569\t    pub fn verify_retry_token(\n570\t        &mut self,\n571\t        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n572\t        _current_time: Instant,\n573\t        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n574\t        _initial_pn: u32,\n575\t        _token: &[u8],\n576\t        _check_reuse: bool,\n577\t    ) -> Result<VerifiedRetryToken, Error> {\n578\t        todo!()\n579\t    }\n580\t}\n581\t\n582\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n583\t// Hash helpers exposed so applications don't need tls.h.\n584\t\n585\t/// Maximum digest size across the hash algorithms tls supports.\n586\t/// C: `HASH_SIZE_MAX`.\n587\tpub const HASH_SIZE_MAX: usize = 64;\n588\t\n589\t/// Construct a streaming hash context for the named algorithm\n590\t/// (e.g. `\"sha256\"`).  Returns a `digest::DynDigest` trait\n591\t/// object so callers feed bytes via `update` and read out via\n592\t/// `finalize` (the canonical Rust Crypto shape).\n593\t///\n594\t/// Backends supply concrete digest types (e.g. `sha2::Sha256`)\n595\t/// and lift them through this entry point.\n596\tpub fn hash_create(_algorithm_name: &str) -> Option<Box<dyn digest::DynDigest>> {\n597\t    todo!()\n598\t}\n599\t\n600\t/// Digest length (bytes) of the named hash algorithm, or 0 when\n601\t/// the algorithm is unknown.  C: `hash_get_length`.\n602\tpub fn hash_get_length(_algorithm_name: &str) -> usize {\n603\t    todo!()\n604\t}\n605\t\n606\t// `hash_update` and `hash_finalize` are gone -- callers use\n607\t// `digest::DynDigest`'s `update` and `finalize_into` methods on\n608\t// the boxed trait object returned by `hash_create`.\n609\t\n610\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n611\t// Private-key / certificate file loaders.\n612\t\n613\timpl Quic {\n614\t    /// Load a PEM-encoded private key from `file_name` and install\n615\t    /// it in this context's master TLS context.  C:\n616\t    /// `set_private_key_from_file`.\n617\t    pub fn set_private_key_from_file(&mut self, _file_name: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n618\t        todo!()\n619\t    }\n620\t}\n621\t\n622\t/// Load a PEM-encoded certificate chain from `file_name` and\n623\t/// return it as an owned vector of DER-encoded certificate byte\n624\t/// strings.  C: `get_certs_from_file` (which allocated both the\n625\t/// outer iovec array and each `base` slot — the Rust shape owns\n626\t/// both via the nested `Vec<Vec<u8>>`).  Returns `None` when the\n627\t/// loader callback is unset or the file fails to parse.\n628\tpub fn get_certs_from_file(_file_name: &str) -> Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>> {\n629\t    todo!()\n630\t}\n631\t\n632\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n633\t// Retry-packet integrity protection.\n634\t//\n635\t// These manage a small set of AEAD contexts (one per supported\n636\t// QUIC version) used to compute retry-packet integrity tags.\n637\t\n638\t/// Build a retry-protection AEAD context from the retry integrity\n639\t/// key for a given version.  C: `create_retry_protection_context`.\n640\tpub fn create_retry_protection_context(\n641\t    _is_enc: bool,\n642\t    _key: &[u8],\n643\t    _prefix_label: &str,\n644\t) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n645\t    todo!()\n646\t}\n647\t\n648\timpl Quic {\n649\t    /// Look up (and lazily create) the retry-protection context for\n650\t    /// the chosen QUIC version, on the chosen direction.  C:\n651\t    /// `find_retry_protection_context`.\n652\t    pub fn find_retry_protection_context(\n653\t        &mut self,\n654\t        _version_index: i32,\n655\t        _sending: bool,\n656\t    ) -> Option<&mut dyn crate::tls::PacketKey> {\n657\t        todo!()\n658\t    }\n659\t\n660\t    /// Tear down every retry-protection AEAD context held by this\n661\t    /// context.  C: `delete_retry_protection_contexts`.\n662\t    pub fn delete_retry_protection_contexts(&mut self) {\n663\t        todo!()\n664\t    }\n665\t}\n666\t\n667\t/// Append the integrity tag to the bytes already written into the\n668\t/// retry packet buffer.  Returns the new write index.  C:\n669\t/// `encode_retry_protection`.\n670\tpub fn encode_retry_protection(\n671\t    _integrity_aead: &dyn crate::tls::PacketKey,\n672\t    _bytes: &mut [u8],\n673\t    _byte_index: usize,\n674\t    _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n675\t) -> usize {\n676\t    todo!()\n677\t}\n678\t\n679\t/// Verify the integrity tag at the end of an inbound retry packet.\n680\t/// Returns the new payload length (with the tag stripped).\n681\t/// C: `verify_retry_protection`.\n682\tpub fn verify_retry_protection(\n683\t    _integrity_aead: &dyn crate::tls::PacketKey,\n684\t    _bytes: &mut [u8],\n685\t    _length: usize,\n686\t    _byte_index: usize,\n687\t    _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n688\t) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n689\t    todo!()\n690\t}\n691\t\n692\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n693\t// Cipher-suite accessors and ECB cipher for CID encryption.\n694\t\n695\t// The cipher-suite lookup functions (`get_cipher_suite_by_id_v`,\n696\t// `get_aes128gcm_sha256_v`, `get_aes128gcm_v`, `ecb_create_by_name`)\n697\t// returned opaque `*mut c_void` cipher-suite handles in C.  In\n698\t// Phase 2 the cipher-suite registry lives on the\n699\t// `crate::tls::TlsBackend` and is reached through\n700\t// `Session::next_1rtt_keys` etc., which return typed\n701\t// `KeyPair`s.  The free-function lookups disappear.\n702\t\n703\t/// AES-128-ECB cipher context.  Phase 2: the C `*mut c_void`\n704\t/// wrapper is gone — this struct now owns a concrete\n705\t/// [`aes::Aes128`] (the Rust Crypto AES implementation).  Used\n706\t/// by the load-balancer CID-encryption flow in [`crate::lb`];\n707\t/// header protection has its own AES paths in\n708\t/// [`crate::header_protection`].\n709\t///\n710\t/// `Direction` distinguishes encrypt-mode from decrypt-mode (the\n711\t/// C side took an `is_enc` flag); each backing AES type\n712\t/// implements one direction only.\n713\tpub enum Aes128EcbContext {\n714\t    Encrypt(aes::Aes128Enc),\n715\t    Decrypt(aes::Aes128Dec),\n716\t}\n717\t\n718\timpl core::fmt::Debug for Aes128EcbContext {\n719\t    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut core::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> core::fmt::Result {\n720\t        f.debug_struct(\"Aes128EcbContext\").finish_non_exhaustive()\n721\t    }\n722\t}\n723\t\n724\timpl Aes128EcbContext {\n725\t    /// Construct an AES-128-ECB context (encrypt or decrypt) keyed\n726\t    /// by `ecb_key`.  C: `aes128_ecb_create`.\n727\t    pub fn new(_is_enc: bool, _ecb_key: &[u8; 16]) -> Self {\n728\t        todo!()\n729\t    }"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0fe661b3-3da3-4093-b2dc-59d86edd2999","uuid":"20a5bf5d-1ea5-4608-b876-68ec7f86bc4b","timestamp":"2026-05-04T21:47:28.032Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tls_api.rs","content":"pub const QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN: usize = 16;\n\n// The AEAD wrappers (`aead_encrypt_generic`, `aead_decrypt_generic`,\n// `aead_encrypt_mp` / `aead_decrypt_mp`, `aead_integrity_limit`,\n// `aead_confidentiality_limit`) are gone -- their behaviour is the\n// `crate::tls::PacketKey` trait.  Backends supply concrete\n// implementations.  Multipath variants fold into the same trait by\n// XORing the path id into the nonce inside the implementor.\n//\n// `aead_free` and `cipher_free` are gone -- `Drop` on the boxed\n// trait object replaces them.\n//\n// `pn_iv_size` and `pn_encrypt` are gone -- header protection\n// (the only consumer) is implemented in `crate::header_protection`\n// and exposed through `crate::tls::HeaderKey`.\n\n// --------------------------