{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C21boFpvoqtkRKBarvMyND","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/tls_api.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! `tls_api.h` is the boundary between quic-core's QUIC machinery\n4\t//! and the TLS 1.3 stack underneath.  It exposes the master\n5\t//! TLS context lifecycle, per-connection TLS contexts, the\n6\t//! retry-token / retry-protection helpers, and a handful of\n7\t//! provider-installation entry points kept in this header so\n8\t//! applications don't have to include `tls.h`.\n9\t//!\n10\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n11\t//! Phase 4 fills in the bodies.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! ## Shape conventions\n14\t//!\n15\t//! * Free C functions whose first argument is a `Quic*` or `Connection*`\n16\t//!   become inherent methods on [`Quic`] / [`Connection`].\n17\t//! * The C `void*` AEAD / PN-encryption / cipher / hash contexts\n18\t//!   are gone — Phase 2 replaced them with the trait family in\n19\t//!   [`crate::tls`] (`PacketKey`, `HeaderKey`, `Session`,\n20\t//!   `ClientConfig`, `ServerConfig`).  Backend implementations\n21\t//!   (picotls, rustls, OpenSSL) wrap their native session types\n22\t//!   to satisfy those traits.  No `unsafe fn` or raw-pointer\n23\t//!   parameters remain in this module.\n24\t//! * `int` flag parameters that encode booleans (`is_enc`,\n25\t//!   `is_client`, `client_mode`, `use_low_memory`, `reset`,\n26\t//!   `check_reuse`, `sending`) become `bool`.\n27\t//! * C status-code returns (`int` 0/-1 / `ERROR_*`) become\n28\t//!   `Result<T, Error>`.  The `SIZE_MAX` failure sentinel returned\n29\t//!   by `aead_decrypt_*` becomes `Err(Error::AeadCheck)` instead of\n30\t//!   leaking the sentinel through the success arm.\n31\t//! * Owning `PtlsIovec* get_certs_from_file(…, size_t* count)`\n32\t//!   collapses to `Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>>`: the C callee `malloc`s\n33\t//!   the slot array and writes its length through the out-pointer,\n34\t//!   and the caller `free`s.  A Rust `Vec` carries both ownership\n35\t//!   and length, with `Drop` taking the place of the manual free.\n36\t//! * Output parameters (`uint8_t reset_secret[16]`,\n37\t//!   `uint8_t* master_secret`, `void** aead_ctx`, `size_t*\n38\t//!   text_length`, `int* data_consumed`, `int* is_new_token`,\n39\t//!   `ConnectionId* odcid`, `size_t* token_size`, `size_t* length`)\n40\t//!   become `&mut`-borrowed slots or are returned as part of the\n41\t//!   function's result tuple, depending on whether the caller\n42\t//!   already holds storage.\n43\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` arguments map to [`core::net::SocketAddr`]\n44\t//!   for parity with `quic.rs`.\n45\t//! * `FreeVerifyCertificateCtx` is a function-pointer typedef in\n46\t//!   `quic.h`; the trait counterpart is already in `quic.rs` and is\n47\t//!   reused here.\n48\t//! * Function-pointer parameters in callbacks\n49\t//!   ([`Quic::tls_set_verify_certificate_callback`]) take\n50\t//!   `Option<Box<dyn …>>` — the C `NULL` sentinel maps to `None`\n51\t//!   and the registered callback is owned by the registry.\n52\t//!\n53\t//! ## Initial-secret label constants\n54\t//!\n55\t//! `LABEL_QUIC_BASE` is `#define`d as `NULL` in C — it's the \"no\n56\t//! prefix label\" sentinel passed to tls.  In Rust the prefix-label\n57\t//! parameters that accept it become `Option<&str>`, so the constant\n58\t//! itself doesn't appear; the call sites pass `None` when they would\n59\t//! have passed `LABEL_QUIC_BASE`.\n60\t//!\n61\t//! ## Public vs. internal `set_*` methods\n62\t//!\n63\t//! `picoquic.h` exposes `picoquic_set_verify_certificate_callback`,\n64\t//! `picoquic_set_client_authentication`, and\n65\t//! `picoquic_set_use_exporter` as thin wrappers around the\n66\t//! `picoquic_tls_set_*` functions declared here.  The wrappers live\n67\t//! on [`Quic`] without the `tls_` prefix (see `lib.rs`); the\n68\t//! TLS-side methods in this module keep the `tls_` prefix so both\n69\t//! pairs can coexist.\n70\t//!\n71\t//! ## Out of scope\n72\t//!\n73\t//! * The five `#if 0`-disabled `cid_*_under_mask_ctx` /\n74\t//!   `cid_free_encrypt_global_ctx` entries are dead code in the C\n75\t//!   source and are not translated.\n76\t//! * `get_private_key_from_file` is also `#if 0` in the header (and\n77\t//!   the `_t` callback variant lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`);\n78\t//!   not translated.\n79\t\n80\textern crate alloc;\n81\t\n82\tuse alloc::boxed::Box;\n83\tuse alloc::vec::Vec;\n84\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n85\t\n86\tuse crate::Instant;\n87\tuse crate::internal::CryptoContext;\n88\tuse crate::{Connection, ConnectionId, Error, Quic, RESET_SECRET_SIZE};\n89\t\n90\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n91\t// Label constants (C `#define` → `&str`).\n92\t//\n93\t// All labels are NUL-terminated bytes in the C header because tls\n94\t// takes them as `char const*`.  The Rust shape is plain `&str`; the\n95\t// trailing NUL is reintroduced (if needed) at the tls FFI\n96\t// boundary.\n97\t\n98\t/// Initial secret label for the client direction.  C:\n99\t/// `LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT`.\n100\tpub const LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT: &str = \"client in\";"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0fe661b3-3da3-4093-b2dc-59d86edd2999","uuid":"4f2f18d3-2511-4ad4-bbac-e786f3f7ba00","timestamp":"2026-05-04T21:47:48.754Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tls_api.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/tls_api.h`.\n//!\n//! `tls_api.h` is the boundary between quic-core's QUIC machinery\n//! and the TLS 1.3 stack underneath.  It exposes the master\n//! TLS context lifecycle, per-connection TLS contexts, the\n//! retry-token / retry-protection helpers, and a handful of\n//! provider-installation entry points kept in this header so\n//! applications don't have to include `tls.h`.\n//!\n//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n//! Phase 4 fills in the bodies.\n//!\n/