ns of every entry in\n//!   `picoquic_t/picoquic_t.c`'s `test_table[]`.  The bare-stub\n//!   shape (one `#[test]` with a `todo!()` body) is generated by\n//!   `scripts/phase3.py`; the body translation lands incrementally\n//!   per file.\n\n// Test infrastructure.\npub mod dualq;\npub mod util;\n\n// Generated test-case modules (one per picoquictest/*.c).\nmod ack_frequency;\nmod ack_of_ack;\nmod app_limited;\nmod bytestream;\nmod cert_verify;\nmod cleartext_aead;\nmod cnx_creation;\nmod cnxstress;\nmod code_version;","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":78}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01FbW8cEYuwnGWTWBcgBGiAC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. So the tests are in `rs/fq/src/tests/` and the test modules are inside the `tests` module which is inside the crate. Since `Connection::crypto_context` is `pub` in `internal.rs`, tests inside the crate can access it.\n\nNow I understand the full picture. Let me plan the translation:\n\n1. **`clear_text_aead()`**: \n   - Create client and server Quic contexts\n   - Create connections\n   - Start client with `cnx.start_client()`\n   - Create server connection\n   - Verify initial CIDs match\n   - Build a packet using helper functions\n   - Encrypt with the client's crypto context and decrypt with the server's, then verify the round-trip works\n   \n   The packet building helpers are internal to the test and use `PacketHeader`, so I'll translate them as Rust helper functions. The `PacketKey::encrypt` method takes a mutable payload vector and appends the authentication tag in-place, so I need to account for that behavior in the translation. is different from the C `aead_encrypt_generic` which takes separate in/out buffers.\n\nActually, looking at the C implementation, it encrypts data in place by taking the output buffer at an offset, the input buffer at an offset with a specific length, the sequence number, the packet header, and the encryption context. The Rust `PacketKey::encrypt` method works similarly but takes the payload as a mutable vector that gets encrypted in place with the tag appended, so I'd need to clone the clear text, extract the payload portion, and call encrypt with the sequence number and header slice.\n\nFor the `pn_ctr()` test, it's doing low-level CTR cipher operations that don't map to any existing Rust API.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_read_input_tokens":8