{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YcERi8x59PF9jEvhXsMymA","type":"tool_result","content":"264\t/// (or ChaCha20) ECB-style transform: feed it a 16-byte sample of\n265\t/// ciphertext and it returns a 16-byte mask whose first 5 bytes\n266\t/// XOR into the protected header.  The trait surface here is\n267\t/// exactly that bare cipher op — picoquic-side logic\n268\t/// ([`crate::header_protection`]) handles sampling, slicing the\n269\t/// header, and applying the mask.\n270\tpub trait HeaderKey: Send {\n271\t    /// Apply the ECB-style block to `sample`, producing the 16-byte\n272\t    /// header-protection mask.  RFC 9001 §5.4.\n273\t    fn mask(&self, sample: [u8; 16]) -> [u8; 16];\n274\t}\n275\t\n276\t/// Bundle of keys associated with a single epoch.\n277\tpub struct Keys {\n278\t    pub header: KeyPairHeader,\n279\t    pub packet: KeyPair,\n280\t}\n281\t\n282\t/// Pair of header-protection keys.\n283\tpub struct KeyPairHeader {\n284\t    pub local: Box<dyn HeaderKey>,\n285\t    pub remote: Box<dyn HeaderKey>,\n286\t}\n287\t\n288\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n289\t// Side-channel data surfaced through `Session`.\n290\t\n291\t/// Server-side handshake data.  Available after the first\n292\t/// ClientHello arrives.\n293\tpub struct HandshakeData {\n294\t    /// SNI (server-name-indication) value from the ClientHello.\n295\t    pub sni: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n296\t    /// Negotiated ALPN value, if any.\n297\t    pub alpn: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n298\t}\n299\t\n300\t/// Peer identity (typically the certificate chain).\n301\tpub enum PeerIdentity {\n302\t    /// X.509 certificate chain.  Each element is one DER-encoded\n303\t    /// certificate.\n304\t    Certificates(Vec<Vec<u8>>),\n305\t}\n306\t\n307\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n308\t// Application-supplied callbacks.\n309\t\n310\t/// Hooks the TLS layer invokes on the application.  One trait\n311\t/// covers ALPN selection, the resumption-ticket store, and\n312\t/// certificate verification.  Default implementations cover\n313\t/// the simple cases; override for custom behavior."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0fe661b3-3da3-4093-b2dc-59d86edd2999","uuid":"22064ca0-050b-4f8e-a0c2-84d218447c1a","timestamp":"2026-05-04T21:49:01.036Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tls.rs","content":"/// (or ChaCha20) ECB-style transform: feed it a 16-byte sample of\n/// ciphertext and it returns a 16-byte mask whose first 5 bytes\n/// XOR into the protected header.  The trait surface here is\n/// exactly that bare cipher op — picoquic-side logic\n/// ([`crate::header_protection`]) handles sampling, slicing the\n/// header, and applying the mask.\npub trait HeaderKey: Send {\n    /// Apply the ECB-style block to `sample`, producing the 16-byte\n    /// header-protection mask.  RFC 9001 §5.4.\n    fn mask(&self, sample: [u8; 16]) -> [u8; 16];\n}\n\n/// Bundle of keys associated with a single epoch.\npub struct Keys {\n    pub header: KeyPairHeader,\n    pub packet: KeyPair,\n}\n\n/// Pair of header-protection keys.\npub struct KeyPairHeader {\n    pub local: Box<dyn HeaderKey>,\n    pub remote: Box<dyn HeaderKey>,\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Side-channel data surfaced through `Session`.\n\n/// Server-side handshake data.  Available after the first\n/// ClientHello arrives.\npub struct HandshakeData {\n    /// SNI (server-name-indication) value from the ClientHello.\n    pub sni: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n    /// Negotiated ALPN value, if any.\n    pub alpn: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n}\n\n/// Peer identity (typically the certificate chain).\npub enum PeerIdentity {\n    /// X.509 certificate chain.  Each element is one DER-encoded\n    /// certificate.\n    Certificates(Vec<Vec<u8>>),\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Application-supplied callbacks.\n\n/// Hooks the TLS layer invokes on the application.  One trait\n/// covers ALPN selection, the resumption-ticket store, and\n/// certificate verification.  Default implementations cover\n/// the simple cases; override for custom behavior.","numLines":50,"startLine":264,"totalLines":359}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01FbFi6a7YMYC82LP8Xefsyj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `HeaderKey::mask` takes a 16-byte sample and returns a 16-byte mask. This is the only operation. The C test's `ptls_cipher_init` / `ptls_cipher_encrypt` is the raw CTR cipher - more low-level than `HeaderKey::mask`.\n\nThe `pn_ctr_test` in C is:\n1. Getting an AES128-GCM AEAD algorithm\n2. Creating a raw CTR cipher context from it \n3. Testing the raw CTR encryption\n\nThis test doesn't map to any Rust API function directly. The closest would be to add a function `aes128_ctr_encrypt(key: &[u8; 16], iv: &[u8; 16], input: &[u8]) -> Vec<u8>` but that seems very low-level.\n\n\nThe C test actually exercises the CTR cipher with variable input sizes—1, 2, 4, 8, and 16 bytes in sequence—whereas `HeaderKey::mask` is hardcoded to work with exactly 16-byte samples, so they're testing different aspects of the same underlying operation. The C test is checking AES-128 CTR encryption of variable-length inputs (not just 16 bytes) against expected values. My `HeaderKey::mask` method only handles exactly 16 bytes, so I need to figure out how to support the variable-length encryption cases in the Rust test. Looking at the test more carefully, it's verifying that encrypting input bytes produces output that matches the XOR of those bytes with expected values, which is the core behavior I need to replicate in the Rust version.\n\nThen it re-encrypts the encrypted bytes to confirm they decrypt back to the original, and finally it tests the actual packet number encryption by encrypting 4 bytes of the packet number and comparing against the expected encrypted result.\n\nFor the Rust side, `HeaderKey::mask` returns a 16-byte mask from a sample, which we'd XOR with the packet number bytes, but that doesn't fully capture what the C test is doing. I think the right approach is to expose a lower-level CTR encryption function in the Rust API that matches the C semantics more directly, or I could just call the intended method and let it panic if it doesn't exist yet—that documents what Phase 4 needs to implement. Looking at the C test implementation, it's creating a cipher directly from the raw key using the TLS API rather than going through the derived secret path. I need to figure out if there's a way to construct a `HeaderKey` from a raw key directly for testing purposes, or if I should just manually apply the mask operation to match what the C test is doing. I'm realizing that `HeaderKey::mask` is essentially performing AES-ECB encryption on the sample, which means I can directly map the C code's pattern of initializing a cipher with an IV and encrypting zeros to a simple call to `mask` with that IV as the input.\n\nFor the packet number encryption part, I'd apply the same masking approach to the packet number bytes and XOR the result with the actual packet number field.\n\nThe key insight is that I need to construct a `HeaderKey` from the raw encryption key to make this translation work cleanly. The guide is clear that I can't use `todo!()` placeholders unless the C code is a no-op or gated by `#if 0`, and since `pn_ctr_test` isn't gated, I need to actually implement the body. I'm thinking about adding a `test_pn_enc_from_raw_key` function to create a `HeaderKey` directly from a raw 16-byte key for this test, and I'm wondering if there's an existing `Aes128EcbContext` type I could leverage.\n\nLooking at the ECB context structure, it's designed for ECB mode rather than CTR, but since the C test only encrypts a single block of zeros, ECB and CTR would produce identical results for that case. The real test is validating three things: that the AES-128 CTR keystream matches the expected output, that encryption and decryption roundtrip correctly, and that packet number encryption works as intended.\n\nThe `Aes128EcbContext` doesn't expose an encrypt method yet—just the constructor—but I can follow the guidance to call the intended method anyway and let it panic if needed, which documents what the API should eventually support. Rather than getting stuck on implementation details, I'll use `AesHeaderProtector` from `header_protection.rs` or call the encrypt method directly on the context, treating the missing implementation as something Phase 4 will handle.\n\nFor the tests, I need to decide between a low-level approach that creates a raw CTR cipher from a key, or using the higher-level `HeaderKey` interface. I'll add a stub function `test_pn_enc_from_raw_key` to `tls_api.rs` that returns a `Box<dyn HeaderKey>` and use that in the test. For the cleartext test, I can access the packet number encryption directly through `Connection`'s public `crypto_context` field and its `CryptoContext` members.\n\nWriting test code... Setting up test addresses and creating a client connection with specific connection IDs to establish the initial state for the QUIC handshake test.\n\nWait, I'm running into a borrowing issue here—after creating the client connection and extracting its initial connection ID, I can't simultaneously hold a mutable reference to the client while also calling methods on the server. Let me check how the existing test utilities handle this pattern, particularly looking at how they manage connection creation and ID passing between client and server. I need to work around Rust's borrowing rules by extracting the client's initial connection ID before creating the server connection, then accessing both connections' crypto contexts separately for the encryption and decryption operations. I'm realizing I need to look at how the existing test translations handle connection access patterns, particularly in `cert_verify.rs`, to understand the right approach for managing these borrowed references.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":712,"cache_read_inpu